Where [See it live](showcase.tmd) shows one capability at a time, the gallery shows
**entire projects**, each a real `.tmd` source tree rendered by Taliesin.

## Probabilistic Modeling: a short course

A lecturer's interactive lecture notes. A numbered **book** whose theorems and proofs
number and cross-reference **across chapters**, a line-by-line **code walkthrough** of
the EM update, and an executable cell: the whole authoring surface a course needs, in
one project.

[Open the course &rarr;](gallery/course/)

## Tarn: a library documentation site

The docs for a small dataframe library. A numbered **book** split into a Guide and an
API **Reference**, with **tabbed** install and usage panels (per package-manager and
per-OS), a line-by-line **code walkthrough** of the core query, version and deprecation
callouts, and full-text **Cmd-K search** that spans every page: the whole shape of a real
open-source documentation site.

[Open the docs &rarr;](gallery/tarn/)

## Gradient descent, by hand

An interactive explainer, the kind you scroll and poke at. One long page where a
**reactive graphic** redraws as you drag the starting point and work three sliders, a
**scrollytelling** walk-through whose figure changes step by step, a live **Observable
Plot** chart wired to the same controls, and the derivation in real **math** alongside:
an explorable explanation with every moving part authored in `.tmd`.

[Open the explainer &rarr;](gallery/descent/)

## Queue latency, Q3: a computational report

A quarterly analytics readout, and the only exhibit here whose pages **run code** as
they build. The data is cleaned and charted in **Python**, the model is fitted in **R**,
and both languages execute in the **same document** against one committed dataset. The
figures and coefficient tables are generated by the report, numbered in document order
across both languages, and cross-referenced from the prose and from a second page.

[Open the readout &rarr;](gallery/analyst/)

## Live 3-D graphics

Interactive graphics that run **client-side in the built page**: a ball-and-stick
**molecule** viewer you can spin and fullscreen, a **Lorenz attractor** drawn as it
integrates, a **CAD** model loaded beside a spur gear the document *computes* from
three numbers, and seven **sorting algorithms** replayed from their real traces.
Every moving part is authored in `.tmd` with `{js}` cells.

[Open the graphics &rarr;](gallery/graphics3d/)
